-- Rewrites the DJI UAV project so every figure and claim matches the final paper:
-- "Demonstrating the Efficiency of RKO-LIO on a DJI UAV Platform Utilizing Drone Built-in IMU for GPS-Denied Mapping".
-- Keeps the "Flight-test results" heading and the figure-005 cover image, which the test suite depends on.

update public.projects set
  subtitle = 'A cost-effective DJI Matrice M350 RTK mapping payload that relies only on the drone''s built-in IMU, validated in a 458.49 m outdoor flight.',
  summary = 'A UAV payload combining a DJI Matrice M350 RTK, an NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX and a Velodyne VLP-16, fusing LiDAR with the aircraft''s built-in 200 Hz IMU through RKO-LIO. The onboard map reached 1.57% average structural error, and relaxing the association gate cut the landing-point error from 1.58 m to 0.31 m.',
  description = $description$This project integrates a DJI Matrice M350 RTK with an NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX (16 GB) and a Velodyne VLP-16 LiDAR to build a UAV mapping payload for GPS-denied operation. Instead of adding an external IMU such as a MicroStrain or Pixhawk Cube Orange, the payload reads the drone's own high-precision IMU, nominally streaming at 200 Hz, through the DJI Payload SDK and the E-Port. This removes the cost, weight and calibration burden of a dedicated inertial unit.

The mapping method is RKO-LIO, a scan-to-map LiDAR-inertial odometry approach that replaces sensor-specific noise modelling with a simplified IMU motion model and an adaptive gravity-orientation regularizer. It was evaluated on a 458.49 m outdoor flight that reached 38.28 m altitude, with swaying trees, complex facades and moving vehicles in the scene.

Three findings came out of the flight. The onboard map reached an average structural error of 1.57% against laser-rangefinder measurements of six facade segments. The default 0.5 m association threshold proved conservative for a 16-beam LiDAR at altitude, and relaxing it lowered the final landing-point error from 1.58 m to 0.31 m. The built-in IMU was also characterised, and it reaches ROS 2 in bursts rather than at a uniform 200 Hz.$description$,
  status = 'Research prototype',
  tags = array['UAV','RKO-LIO','LiDAR-Inertial Odometry','ROS 2','Sensor Fusion','GPS-denied Navigation','DJI E-Port','Edge Computing'],
  tools = array['DJI Matrice M350 RTK','NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX','Velodyne VLP-16','ROS 2 Humble','DJI Payload SDK','RKO-LIO','UART','USB Bulk','DC-DC Power'],
  outcomes = array[
    '1.57% average structural error against laser-rangefinder measurements (maximum 2.47%)',
    '458.49 m outdoor flight reaching 38.28 m altitude',
    'Landing-point error cut from 1.58 m to 0.31 m (0.343% to 0.067% of path length)',
    '25.79% average CPU and 1.99 GB RAM on the Jetson Orin NX',
    '5,673.91 m² mapped area and 190,343 m³ bounding volume from the onboard map',
    '12.2% of built-in IMU samples arrive after gaps above 15 ms'
  ],
  rich_content = $rich$<h2>Project objective</h2><p>This project builds a low-cost, low-weight UAV mapping payload for environments where GPS cannot be relied on. The design uses a Velodyne VLP-16 LiDAR and the DJI Matrice M350 RTK's own built-in IMU, nominally streaming at 200 Hz, so no external inertial unit is needed. RKO-LIO is then evaluated on this aerial platform in a dynamic outdoor flight.</p><figure><img src="https://engportfolio-zhkmdjuy.manus.space/manus-storage/figure-005_82c208cf.jpg" alt="Comparison of the surveyed outdoor area and reconstructed coloured point cloud" /><figcaption>Surveyed outdoor area alongside the reconstructed coloured point cloud from the UAV mapping experiment.</figcaption></figure><h2>Payload and integration</h2><ul><li><strong>Airframe:</strong> DJI Matrice M350 RTK, rated for a 2.7 kg payload and IP55, with an E-Port interface for power and data.</li><li><strong>Edge compute:</strong> NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX (16 GB) running Ubuntu 22.04 and ROS 2 Humble.</li><li><strong>LiDAR:</strong> Velodyne VLP-16, 360-degree coverage at 10 Hz, connected over Ethernet and powered through a dedicated voltage step-down circuit.</li><li><strong>Built-in IMU:</strong> read from the flight controller over the native telemetry link, so no separate clock synchronisation with an external IMU is required.</li><li><strong>Communication:</strong> USB Bulk carries the high-bandwidth data from the E-Port to the Jetson, while UART at 230400 bps carries the IMU stream, drone control and take-off coordinates.</li><li><strong>Software:</strong> a ROS 2 stack with a strict TF tree, where <code>base_link</code> is the drone's centre of mass and the LiDAR frame is defined from millimetre measurements of its mounting position.</li></ul><h2>Why RKO-LIO and its association gate</h2><p>RKO-LIO replaces sensor-specific noise modelling with a kinematic motion model and an adaptive gravity-orientation regularizer that is automatically de-weighted when the platform vibrates or accelerates hard. Registration is point-to-point against a VDB voxel map, and outliers such as moving vehicles and swaying foliage are rejected by a fixed association gate.</p><p>The VLP-16's adjacent beams are 2 degrees apart, so at range R they land R·tan(2°) apart. That exceeds the default 0.5 m gate beyond about 14.3 m, and 52.8% of the returns in this flight lie beyond that range. This is why the gate became the key parameter to study.</p><h2>Flight-test results</h2><ul><li><strong>Trajectory:</strong> 458.49 m over 484.21 s, with 4,802 LiDAR scans, an average speed of 0.95 m/s, a peak of 3.95 m/s and a maximum altitude of 38.28 m in the complete reconstruction.</li><li><strong>Structural accuracy:</strong> six facade segments were measured with laser rangefinders and compared with the onboard map. The average relative error was 1.57%, the maximum 2.47% and the minimum 0.95%.</li><li><strong>Coverage of the onboard map:</strong> 5,673.91 m² of 2D bounding-box area and 190,343 m³ of 3D bounding-box volume.</li><li><strong>Landing-point error:</strong> measured against a physically surveyed landing point, the error fell from 3.489 m at a 0.25 m gate, to 1.583 m at the 0.5 m default, to 0.489 m at 1.0 m, and to 0.307 m at 5.0 m. That is 0.710%, 0.343%, 0.107% and 0.067% of path length.</li><li><strong>Rejected correspondences:</strong> 14.5% at 0.25 m, 2.0% at 0.5 m, 0.4% at 1.0 m and 0.1% at 5.0 m.</li><li><strong>Edge resource use:</strong> an average of 25.79% CPU (peak 31.9%) and about 1.99 GB RAM (peak 2.01 GB), with CPU and GPU temperatures averaging 56.47 °C and 53.94 °C under a 15 W profile.</li></ul><blockquote>Relaxing the association gate from 0.5 m to 5.0 m reduced the final landing-point error from 1.58 m to 0.31 m over the 458.49 m flight.</blockquote><h2>Real-time behaviour and IMU transport</h2><ul><li><strong>Frame loss:</strong> the onboard pipeline processed 2,819 of 4,802 scans, so 41.3% were skipped. The mean cost per scan was 80.5 ms, inside the 100 ms sensor period, but latency spikes (95th percentile 179.8 ms) with a subscriber queue one element deep caused the next scan to be dropped. The onboard map still reached the 1.57% structural error.</li><li><strong>IMU timestamps:</strong> the 96,669 samples average 5.01 ms apart, matching 200 Hz, but they arrive in bursts. 66.2% of intervals are shorter than 3 ms, and 12.2% of samples follow a gap above 15 ms, the longest being 41.3 ms. This comes from the DJI Payload SDK transport path rather than the sensor itself.</li></ul><h2>Limitations</h2><p>The results come from a single flight. No continuous reference trajectory was available, so absolute trajectory error and relative pose error could not be computed, and the landing-point reference carries about ±0.15 m of uncertainty. The odometry uses no satellite positioning, so the approach is GPS-denied by construction, but GNSS was available at the test site, so operation under real signal loss was not demonstrated.</p><h2>Engineering contribution</h2><p>The contribution is a field-oriented integration that links the DJI platform interfaces, UART and DC-DC power hardware, ROS 2 middleware and LiDAR-inertial estimation on edge hardware, together with a measured characterisation of the built-in IMU's telemetry path. Future work targets using the real-time onboard map for AI-driven environmental analysis without satellite positioning.</p><h2>Credits</h2><p>Authors: Mohammed Ali Mohammed S. Bajhaw, Anas Mahyoub Naji Saeed Alqadhi, Munef el Muhammed and Aysegul Ucar, Mechatronics Engineering Department, Fırat University. Funded by TÜBİTAK (grant 123E406) and the Fırat University Scientific Research Projects Unit (FUBAP, grants MF.24.80 and MF.25.155).</p>$rich$,
  updated_at = now()
where slug = 'rko-lio-dji-uav-gps-denied-mapping';
